    "description": "Chogan is an Iranian equestrian ball game in which mounted teams use wooden sticks to score through opposing goalposts. Its heritage form includes storytelling and music, connecting athletic skill with literature, performance, and the transmission of knowledge.",
    "tags": ["Chogan", "Equestrian sports", "Polo", "Storytelling", "Intangible heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:10:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:10:18Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Chogan: Iran’s Equestrian Game and Living Heritage | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover chogan through its mounted game, music, storytelling, regional variation, and place in Persian cultural history."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "game-and-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A mounted game within a larger performance",
        "content": "Chogan brings together mounted competition, music, and storytelling. Opposing teams move a ball toward goalposts with wooden sticks. UNESCO recognized the Iranian practice in 2017 under the title Chogān, a horse-riding game accompanied by music and storytelling. That wording is significant: the recognized element includes the performers surrounding the game, as well as the riders. [s1]\n\nThe heritage description identifies players, storytellers, and musicians as its principal bearers. Knowledge belongs to people who train horses and riders, but also to those who preserve narratives and musical performance. Understanding chogan solely through its resemblance to polo leaves out this wider cultural setting. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "playing-and-variation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The field, the ball, and local variation",
        "content": "The ball is called the *guy*, and the open playing field the *meydan*. Play is divided into rounds called *chukkeh*. The ichLinks heritage record explicitly notes regional differences in field size, player numbers, and the number of rounds. A single fixed format should therefore not be imposed on every traditional performance. [s2]\n\nThe same record explains practical constraints: the game requires suitable level ground, trained horses, and experienced riders. It associates performances with major occasions, including Nowruz, the Iranian New Year. These requirements help explain why transmission involves families and specialist communities rather than equipment alone. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "literature-and-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A game carried through places and stories",
        "content": "Iran’s former capitals form an important part of the heritage account. The ichLinks record names Qazvin, Isfahan, and Tehran, together with Karaj, in describing communities associated with the tradition. It also acknowledges related practices outside Iran whose performance and social functions differ. These connections place chogan within a regional history without making every related game identical. [s2]\n\nWritten narratives offer another route into its past. Feyzi Halıcı’s encyclopedia entry discusses chogan in Ferdowsi’s *Shahnameh*, the Persian Book of Kings, and in the Middle Persian narrative of Ardashir’s exploits. Such texts establish the game’s importance within literary traditions. Stories about legendary rulers should not, however, be converted into precisely dated sporting results. [s3]\n\nThe entry also traces related names across Persian, Arabic, Turkish, and Greek. That linguistic movement provides a useful reminder that the game’s cultural history crosses modern borders. Its terminology, stories, and forms of play traveled through different courts and societies. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transmission",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What safeguarding needs to preserve",
        "content": "UNESCO describes informal family transmission alongside workshops and associations that support training and local masters. Safeguarding therefore concerns more than organizing matches: it includes maintaining the knowledge needed to perform, narrate, and teach the tradition while respecting local differences. [s1]\n\nFor newcomers, this is the most useful way to read chogan’s heritage status. The athletic contest is central, but the surrounding arts explain why the game also belongs in accounts of literature, community memory, and cultural education. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "heritage-components",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The connected practices of chogan",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Chogan heritage practice\"] -->|\"Includes\"| B[\"Mounted team game\"]\n  A -->|\"Includes\"| C[\"Storytelling\"]\n  A -->|\"Includes\"| D[\"Musical performance\"]\n  E[\"Families and local practitioners\"] -->|\"Transmit knowledge of\"| A\n  F[\"Associations and workshops\"] -->|\"Support teaching of\"| A\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates the game’s three documented components and identifies channels of transmission. The arrows describe inclusion and teaching, not a governing hierarchy.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
